{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested at exact e3725505c798fab88165e1a8b87447cd422feeea, on all three supplied artifacts, primary market note21200f23 first. I read the complete primary note, both supporting diffs and affected case/roadmap contexts, governing request42b9dee8, and actual review prepare: assigned to42b9dee8/010a41a9. This is document review only; it commissions no application.\n\nArchitecture: the proposed distinction between a trusted holder's private decision and a group whose members all replay it is useful. Cases21/32/37/39/40/41 and the private parts of08/18/19/24/25/33 can use that direction when the stated holder is trusted and the other parties receive only the promised disclosure. A citation is not proof that they replayed its private comparison. The market's actual memberships, exported provenance and bounded reads do not yet implement its stated promises. Security: changes required for F1/F2 below. Simplification: retain the plain scope/membership approach and operator reports, but use explicit public exports with safe provenance and acknowledge mechanisms Grow already has; do not add private-view machinery to hide a contradictory membership layout.\n\nF1 [P1] Make the market's membership match its privacy promises (note3:128-160,5:202-220; use case51). All providers join the roster, whose offers contain prices and whose leads contain public post/profile sources. Up to eight competing providers then join one need's engagement room containing every match, its eventual contact exchange and fee. Each such member can read that entire room and its retained history. This contradicts 'a provider never reads ... another provider's price', 'a provider reads their own offer and their own matches', and the losing-provider comparison claim. It also makes pre-enrollment leads visible to already-enrolled providers, despite the opening claim that the record holds only invited/enrolled consented data. Decide what is intentionally public, keep pre-invitation sourcing and private competition in suitable scopes, and give a member/read table that remains true for every competing provider. If competition is private, give each provider a separate scope and keep the shared decision with its trusted owner. Removing a member or closing an item does not retract history already read. Update the claimed buyer-later 'invitation is the only addition' to the resulting disclosure boundary.\n\nF2 [P1] Include retained source-entry bytes in the cross-scope privacy design (note5:213-232 and the new group3/roadmap explanation). A native message does more than put a hash in the receiving room. packages/scope/src/delivery.ts:6-15 reads and retains the full source entry, and reads.ts:42-43,287-303 permits authorized destination readers to read retained entry inputs. Sending the roster update directly from introduce therefore exports the source entry that the note says also writes the private contact exchange/fee. Roster members can read that retained provenance even if the displayed offer contains only a citation. Define a separate minimal public export whose entire retained dependency closure is safe for those readers, or use an explicitly opaque citation with its verification limits. State that a native FactRef is {at:{scope,inc,kind},seq,hash} (contract/scope.ts:61-64), rather than just hash+position; ordinary recorded facts already have it. The sequenced note adds group ordering and effective commit facts, not private disclosure by reference. Apply the export/proof limitation to the blanket group3 and roadmap claims as well.\n\nF3 [P1] Reconcile the roster bounds with the declared projection/card path (note3:180-183 and M1). The first version admits200offers and500leads, but current many-source projections require a complete set within at most100rows (application-projection-types.ts:10, application-projection.ts:97-99). DeclaredViewSource reads the native source before applying the member carousel filter (declared.ts:99-139). A101st matching row makes that source incomplete; an8match or2KiB-per-need limit does not solve it. Name the bounded read/routing/summary design that serves every admitted offer/lead and the site-wide carousel, or lower the admitted populations. Do not imply that client pagination of the existing complete-many read already exists.\n\nF4 [P2] Correct current Grow capability claims in all three documents. The note5/7 and new roadmap/use-cases group text say no declared application has scopes of its own and this market would be first. Grow is already a declared package with Site and Room: examples/grow/site.ts:24-35 creates children and receives room-summary; room.ts:59-62 sends the summary relation, check/dap.application.json packages both definitions, and scope/test/grow-demo-tick.test.ts exercises native creation. Distinguish any genuinely missing per-engagement membership/authority or packaging form from existing create/relation composition; do not present all multi-scope application messaging as unbuilt. In use case46:212-214, 'Live presence keeps no record' contradicts landed G3. examples/grow/presence.ts explicitly defines recorded presence, with arrive/leave transitions and retained reportedAt facts; the G3 delivery note and site test preserve them beyond101later reports. Say what runs, and label a future volatile surface separately. The definition still admits only recorded execution; first sequenced slices do not mean sequenced runtime is available.\n\nF5 [P2] Make the cloud-agent claims dated and no stronger than their evidence (note6/Sources). The Jan26 MCP Apps announcement predates the agent's Sep8launch and is a positive list of supported hosts, not evidence of this later product's current inability to render Apps. The Sep13 UI walkthrough describes a browser but does not test or mention MCP Apps. Change 'shows no MCP Apps views' to a dated unverified/inferred limitation unless an actual capability test or primary statement supports it. Limit 'Meta's own documentation ... does not mention MCP' to the named Sep8safety article; that single article is not all documentation. The cited reports also disagree: Parallel(Sep26) describes writing an SDK bridge and no add-server setting, while AnythingMCP(checkedSep25) describes adding a URL in Settings and OAuth. They are September reports, not 'reports from this month' onOct20. Preserve the distinct reported routes/dates and uncertainty rather than cite both as the same successful client-writing evidence. Meta's current Help Center confirms custom connectors generally, without establishing either exact MCP route. Keep Meta AI Connectors distinct from Muse as the note already does. Readbacks: https://research.meta.ai/blog/security-and-safety-for-ai-agents-our-approach-with-muse ; https://blog.modelcontextprotocol.io/posts/2026-01-26-mcp-apps/ ; https://memeburn.com/meta-muse-web-ui-walkthrough/ ; https://www.meta.com/help/artificial-intelligence/1687253048996149/ . The stale OAuth requestb04b0e79 is now carried by live3f598e55; update the work reference.\n\nValidation: all148relative links/anchors in the three exact-head files resolve; cases01..51 occur exactly once; the old renumbered group-anchor patterns have no remaining references in candidate docs/notes/README; whitespace diff passes. Native artifact bindings and exact-head preparation pass. Evidence is in /private/tmp/checker-market-review-2nc96s4y/link-and-case-check.json. The ordinary gate does not exercise these documentation-only files, so no project suite was run. Public source pages above were read; the YouTube/video-comments endpoint failed to open, so the164-comment/most-liked characterization was not independently verified. No repository source changed, no installation, push, deployment, custody or external message outside this authorized gitseq review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:21200f2349bee45f19181ab8363a88ef9cf7382a","binding":"assigned","head":"e3725505c798